<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malhar Rao Holkar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1693">1693</a>–<a href="/years/1766">1766</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Malhar Rao Holkar I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malhar Rao Holkar was the founder of the Holkar dynasty of Indore. The son of a shepherd, he rose as a cavalry commander under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a> and received lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> from about 1731. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> expansion into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and survived the defeat at Panipat in 1761. His daughter-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahilyabai-holkar">Ahilyabai Holkar</a> succeeded to his domains.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malhar-rao-holkar.jpg" alt="Malhar Rao Holkar" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malhar_Rao_Holkar_contemporary.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Malhar Rao was born on 16 March 1693 in Murum village near Phaltan, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> district, to Khandu Ji Holkar, a deshgavda of a Dhangar family. His father died in 1696, and he grew up at Taloda in the castle of his maternal uncle Sardar Bhojrajrao Bargal, who held a cavalry under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> noble Sardar Kadam Bande and soon gave him charge of a cavalry detachment. In 1717 he married his uncle's daughter Gautama Bai, and between 1719 and 1726 he took three more wives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranoji-scindia">Ranoji Scindia</a> and Anandrao Pawar he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> drive into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-ii">Jai Singh II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> governor of Malwa again in 1732-1733, forces led by Holkar defeated him near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandsaur">Mandsaur</a>, and Jai Singh agreed to pay six lakh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> and cede 28 parganas. Malhar Rao fought at Jalesar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and Bhopal in 1737, helped take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasai">Vasai</a> from the Portuguese in 1739, and from 1748 his position in Malwa was secure.</p><p>He helped Safdarjung defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> and Bangash in 1751-1752. At the siege of Kumher Fort in 1754 his only son Khanderao was killed by a cannonball, but rivalry with Jayappa Sindhia led him to accept a treaty with the Jat Maharaja Suraj Mal on 18 May 1754. With Raghunathrao he routed the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-bahadur">Ahmad Shah Bahadur</a> at the First Battle of Sikandarabad, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> on 11 August 1757 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> on 20 April 1758. The Afghan general Jahan Khan defeated him at the Second Battle of Sikandarabad in 1760. Historians are divided over his failure to aid Dattaji Scindia and his withdrawal from Panipat, which saved thousands of civilians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Panipat he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> forces at the Battle of Mangrol and helped restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> power in Central India, including that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia">Mahadji Scindia</a>. After Khanderao's death he had kept his daughter-in-law Ahilya Bai from committing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a>. He died at Alampur on 20 May 1766. His grandson Male Rao ruled Indore under Ahilyabai's regency but died within months, and she then ruled herself. She built his samadhi chhatri at the place of his cremation at Alampur, in Bhind district, and he is counted among the architects of Maratha control over India.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malhar Rao Holkar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Malhar Rao Holkar</b>, a shepherd's son who rose as a cavalry commander under Peshwa Baji Rao I, received lands in Malwa from about 1731.</blockquote><a href="/state/indore-state">Indore State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jejuri, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.27,74.16&amp;year=1740" aria-label="Jejuri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Malhar Rao Holkar"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6138 5925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6138 5925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.27,74.16&amp;year=1740">What was here in 1740? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
